# Here be how the parser gets a line from the user, and makes sense of it...

# The parser starts out with these variables and resets them to these values:

String activeVerb = null;
List activeNouns = null;        // ArrayList of nouns!
String activeAttr = null;       // Generic adjective "silver" or "rusty"
String activePrep = null;       // Preposition "on, off, on, under" etc.
String activeCateg  = null;     // Type (food, utensil, weapon, key)
Boolean pluralFlag = false;     // True if a plurality trigger is found

String userInput = null;        // raw line of input from user
userTokens = null;              // ArrayList of String
int currentToken = 0;           // Position on userTokens ArrayList

- First it receives a line from the user, such as "Take the silver fork."  This goes into
    the string userInput.

- It mashes case to lower case: https://www.w3schools.com/java/ref_string_tolowercase.asp

- It tokenizes it into an ArrayList (usertokens) using split()
    https://www.w3schools.com/java/ref_string_split.asp

    - currentToken correlates to the ArrayList positions.

- It then steps through the array, testing to see if it finds matching keywords:

  == TOP OF LOOP ==

  - First it checks to see if it is at the end of the ArrayList (currentToken == size of
        Array).  As it's set up now with the currentToken incrementation, an ArrayList of
        4 items (0, 1, 2, 3) will correlate with currentToken being 4 (one past the end).

        - If it is at the end of the Array:
            - It checks that a verb, and at least one of plural, noun, category is set.
              - If not, we hit the end of the line and don't have a complete command.
                1) Print err of confusion
                2) Discard whole line, reset vars, start over.
                    - This error state is handled by showConfusion() method, see below.
                3) Exit parse loop, and wait for next input.

              - If we have a verb and at least one of plural, noun, category by the end,
                    then we have a complete command and there is no more input:
                    1) 1) Load the CommandObject with the verb, noun, attr, prep, categ, pluralflag
                    2) Send the CommandObject to the ActionHandler
                    3) Reset all vars for activeVerb, activeNouns, activeAttr, activePrep, activeCateg
                    4) Reset userInput, userTokens
                        - This sequence is handled by cleanSlate() method, see below.
                    6) Exit parse loop, and wait for next input.

// Verb Check

  - It looks for verbs.
    - If one is found:
        - It checks if activeVerb is set, but activeNouns, activeCat, and pluralFlag are
            not. (Plural flag for "take all").
            - If that is the case it has hit two verbs in a row with nothing to act
              on, and
                1) Prints an error or message of confusion.
                2) Discards the whole line, resets all vars (showConfusion())
                3) Exit parse loop, wait for the next input.


            - If any of activeNouns, activeCateg, or pluralFlag are set, (along with a
              set active verb), that is a complete command and a signal to:
                1) Load the CommandObject with the verb, noun, prep, attr, categ,
                pluralflag
                2) Send the CommandObject to the ActionHandler
                3) Reset all vars for activeVerb, activePrep, activeNouns, activeAttr,
                activeCateg
                4) Increment currentToken by 1
                5) Start on the next command (top of loop).

// Everything else check

    - If a verb is not found:
        1) It checks for a preposition, attribute, plural, noun, pluralNoun, category in
            order.
            - if it finds any of those without an active verb set, it prints an error
                to the effect that it doesn't understand, or "what about <noun>?", or
                "any what?".
                - Also discards whole line, resets vars, starts over. (showConfusion())

            - if it finds any of those WITH an active verb set:
                1) Sets the appropriate var for the item it found
                    - Note: pluralNoun sets the plural flag true also, and sets the noun
                      to the singular form:  forks-->fork.
                2) Increments currentToken by 1
                3) Start at the next command (top of loop).



## Prescribed Methods:

void showConfusion(): For when the sentence can't be parsed, either because it was bad,
    or there was a keyword that should have been there, but wasn't.  How this is handled
    will require some thought, as it's real easy to just say "I don't know wtf you are
    talking about" as a generic (randomly selected or not) message, with no regard to
    what was input, or what part wasn't recognized.

    It would be nice to have this method be a little smarter, in that it gets a copy of
    the input and can give some kind of feedback like "I don't know how to take <word>",
    or "I don't understand what a <word> is, can you try a different word?"

    Even smarter yet would be one where the partial sentence is still retained (held by a
    state change) and the message allows the user to clarify with a short sentence, i.e.:
    "I don't know how to take <word>, do you have another name for it?".  The player then
    can type another word for the item, and if found, the previous word that it didn't
    recognize can get added to the list of names/words that refer to that object. A
    parser that learns!

    Either way, if it doesn't understand it runs cleanSlate() to reset everything at the
    end.


void cleanSlate(): resets ALL variables, and prepares the object for the next line of
    input from the user.  I would call it the happy path but it gets invoked even if the
    parser errors out.  At least in this iteration, if you say something it doesn't
    understand it just balks, throws its hands up in the air, and everything starts over.


void clearPreviousCommand():  resets only activeVerb, activePrep, activeNouns,
    activeAttr, activeCateg and pluralFlag.  All other vars are retained, as this is
    used once a complete command is found and a CommandObject is loaded and sent off.
    For example:

    "Take the forks, and drop the spoon and plate."

    1) Parser loads a CommandObject with:
        verb: take
        pluralFlag: true
        noun: fork

        clearPreviousCommand() is run, and then the loop starts anew on the rest of the
        input line to get:
        verb: drop
        noun: spoon, plate



String matchVerb(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it against the list of
    verbs (and their aliases).  If it finds a match, it sets the active verb, and returns
    a 'success' signal.  If not, it returns null, or something.  Success tells the parser
    to move on to the next token.  Null tells it to try the next match method on the
    current token.

String matchPrep(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it against the list of
    prepositions (on, off, in, out, on, under).  If it finds a match, it sets the active
    prep variable, and returns a 'success' signal.  If not, it returns null, or such.
    Success tells the parser to move on to the next token.  Null tells it to try the
    next match method on the current token.

String matchPlural(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it against the list of
    'plurality triggers', such as 'all, any, every, each'.  If any of these are found, it
    sets the plural flag true, and returns a 'success' signal.  If not, returns null, or
    something.  Null tells the parser to try the next match method on the current token.


String matchNoun(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it against the list of
    nouns and noun aliases.  If any of these are found, it adds it to the activeNouns
    ArrayList, and returns a 'success' signal.  If not, returns null.


String matchPluralNoun(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it against the list
    of plural nouns and plural noun aliases.  This list is identical to the above noun
    list, but instead has plural spellings.  So, forks instead of fork, spoons instead of
    spoon, and so on.  If a match is found, it sets the plural flag to true, adds the
    _singular_ spelling of the noun to the activeNouns ArrayList, and returns a 'success'
    signal.  Null otherwise.


String matchCategory(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it agains the list of
    singular categories (tool, weapon, utensil, etc).  If a match is found, the
    activeCateg variable is set, and a 'success' signal is returned.  Null otherwise.
    Note that some names are inherently plural, like "food" or "dice", and will be in the
    pluralCategory list below.  The reason is we want to trigger the plural flag so that
    all 'food' or all 'dice' are acted upon.

String matchPluralCategory(String token): takes a token (word) and checks it against the
    list of plural categories.  This list is identical to the above category list, but
    instead has plural spellings.  Tools, weapons, food, utensils.  If found, the
    plural flag is set true, and the _singular_ form of the category is set in the
    activeCateg variable.  For inherently plural items "food, dice, water" the standard
    form is set.  Thus, "take food" will take all the food items in the room.

    Also, each actionObject will likely use caution when given the plural flag- if the
    player says "eat food" or "drink water" when carrying multiple items with a plural
    flag set, it will stop, list off all items and say "please name each item you wish to
    eat" or something.  This could go a few different ways:

    The smarter parser will ask "do you mean to eat everything? Type 'list food' to see
    what you're about to eat", then wait for a 'yes' (to go ahead and eat everything) or a 'no' (to say "I didn't think so!") or anything else to discard that line and start
    over.

    The simpler parser might just go "you eat the chorizo, beans, and rice." and that is
    that.


## What is intentionally left out of this parser:

    This iteration won't have any containers either, so it won't have
    the facility to handle "put <item> into <container" or "open <item that is not a
    door>".
